<header class="hero" id="top">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Pharmacy graduation project &middot; Tripoli, Libya</p>
    <h1>Antimicrobial resistance in <i>E. coli</i> from urinary infections</h1>
    <p class="lead">A common gut bacterium is now one of the hardest to treat. This page explains what <i>E. coli</i> is, how it causes urinary tract infections, how resistance looks around the world, and what we found in 20 isolates from Tripoli.</p>
    <p class="hint" style="margin:0">Tap the numbered parts of the cell below to see how each one matters for infection and for antibiotics.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="stage" id="heroStage"></div>
</header>

<section class="block" id="about">
  <h2 class="big">What is <i>E. coli</i>?</h2>
  <p class="kick">Most people carry it every day without harm.</p>
  <div class="cols txt">
    <div class="panel"><p><i>Escherichia coli</i> is a rod-shaped, Gram-negative bacterium about 2 micrometres long. It lives in the intestines of people and animals, where most strains are harmless and some even help keep other microbes in check.</p><p>A few strains carry extra genes that let them cause disease. The ones that infect the urinary tract are called uropathogenic <i>E. coli</i>.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><p>It is the most common cause of urinary tract infections (UTIs), which affect more than 150 million people worldwide every year.<sup>1</sup> Women are affected more often, and 14 of the 20 patients in our study were women.</p><p>Most UTIs are treated with a short course of antibiotics, so resistance to those antibiotics changes what a doctor can prescribe.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><p><b>Why pharmacists care.</b> Every antibiotic choice depends on local resistance. If a drug fails in a large share of local isolates, it should not be the first choice for that community.</p><p>That is the reason local data, even from a small study, is useful.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="anatomy">
  <h2 class="big">Inside one cell</h2>
  <p class="kick">A cut-away of an <i>E. coli</i> cell, with flagella moving and fimbriae swaying. Select a part to isolate it.</p>
  <div class="anat">
    <div class="stage panel" style="padding:8px"><div id="anatStage"></div><p class="hint">Not to scale. Real flagella are several times longer than the cell.</p></div>
    <div class="parts" id="parts"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="uti">
  <h2 class="big">How a urinary infection starts</h2>
  <p class="kick">Four steps, in this order.</p>
  <div class="cols">
    <div class="panel"><ol class="steps">
      <li><div><b>From the gut to the urethra.</b><br>Bacteria that live in the bowel reach the opening of the urinary tract. The female urethra is shorter, which is one reason infections are more common in women.</div></li>
      <li><div><b>Up to the bladder.</b><br>They travel up the urethra and enter the bladder, helped by flagella.</div></li>
      <li><div><b>Sticking on.</b><br>The tip of type 1 fimbriae, a protein called FimH, binds to bladder cells so urine flow does not wash the bacteria away.<sup>1</sup></div></li>
      <li><div><b>Growing and spreading.</b><br>They multiply, can form protective communities called biofilms, and can climb to the kidneys. Untreated, this can lead to a bloodstream infection.</div></li>
    </ol></div>
    <div class="panel txt"><p><b>Where resistance comes in.</b> Many E. coli make enzymes called extended-spectrum &beta;-lactamases (ESBLs) that destroy common antibiotics such as ceftriaxone. A smaller number make carbapenemases (NDM, OXA-48, KPC), which destroy the carbapenems used as a last resort.</p><p>The World Health Organization lists both cephalosporin-resistant and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales in the critical priority group of its 2024 priority pathogens list.</p><p>In our project we looked at the susceptibility result for each isolate and, with real-time PCR, we are testing which resistance genes explain it.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="world">
  <h2 class="big">Resistance around the world</h2>
  <p class="kick">Figures from the 2019 global burden analysis and the 2025 WHO surveillance report.</p>
  <div class="stat4">
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">1 in 6</div><div class="l"><b style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">Resistant infections</b><br>lab-confirmed bacterial infections in 2023 were resistant to treatment (WHO)</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">1.27 M</div><div class="l"><b style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">Deaths caused</b><br>by bacterial resistance in 2019, with 4.95 million more associated with it</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">#1</div><div class="l"><b style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)"><i>E. coli</i> ranks first</b><br>among the six pathogens behind most of these deaths in 2019</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="v">5&ndash;15%</div><div class="l"><b style="font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">Yearly rise</b><br>in over 40% of tracked drug-pathogen pairs, 2018 to 2023 (WHO)</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h2>E. coli resistant to third-generation cephalosporins</h2>
    <div class="cmp" id="cmp"></div>
    <p class="hint">WHO figures are reported as &ldquo;over 40%&rdquo; and &ldquo;above 70%&rdquo;, so the bars show the lower limit. Our figure is ceftriaxone in 20 urinary isolates tested on the Phoenix system, so it is a pilot value and not a comparison with national data. WHO gives no <i>E. coli</i>-specific figure for the Eastern Mediterranean Region, where Libya sits.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="results">
  <h2 class="big">Our results</h2>
  <p class="kick">20 urinary <i>E. coli</i> isolates, coded E1 to E20 with no patient identifiers, tested on the BD Phoenix 50. Pick a group to filter every panel, then click an isolate code to see its profile. Gene results from real-time PCR are being confirmed and will be added.</p>
<div class="bar"><div class="chips" id="chips" role="group" aria-label="Filter isolates"></div></div>

  <div class="tiles" id="tiles"></div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <section class="panel">
      <h2>Resistance by antibiotic</h2>
      <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw R"></i>Resistant</span><span><i class="sw I"></i>Intermediate</span><span id="bn" style="margin-left:auto">&nbsp;</span></div>
      <div id="bars"></div>
    </section>
    <section class="panel detail" id="detail" aria-live="polite"></section>
  </div>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Isolate by antibiotic</h2>
    <div class="legend">
      <span><i class="sw S">S</i>Susceptible</span><span><i class="sw I">I</i>Intermediate</span><span><i class="sw R">R</i>Resistant</span>
      <span><i class="sw X"></i>No category reported</span><span><i class="sw N">&ndash;</i>Not on this panel</span>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll"><table class="hm" id="hm"></table></div>
  </section>

  <div class="note">
    <p>Phoenix reports were not marked final when read. Isolate E18 is resistant to carbapenems and cephalosporins but susceptible to ampicillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate and piperacillin-tazobactam, so its profile needs a repeat from the Phoenix laboratory.</p>
    <p>Percentages use the isolates with a reported category for each antibiotic. Panels 483, 485 and 431 carry different drugs, so the denominators differ. With 20 isolates this is a pilot dataset and not a surveillance estimate.</p>
  </div>
